            <ArticleTitle>Consumption of antibiotics for community-acquired infections by adults in Greece: A cross-sectional study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1741-1743</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0196-6553(16)30527-2</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ajic.2016.04.242</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We studied the rates and characteristics of antibiotics consumption for community-acquired infections in 309 adult patients. Of them, 293 (94.8%) had received at least 1 course of antibiotics during the past year. In total, 419 courses of antibiotics were consumed during the past year, including 285 (68%) following medical examination, 72 (17.2%) following telephone consultation, 30 (7.2%) following suggestion by a pharmacist, and in 16 (3.8%) the antibiotic was available at home. Older age and asthma were significantly associated with a higher risk for antibiotics consumption.</AbstractText>
